212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051078 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051078 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051078 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429233 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051078 #edb/ref 1031 "Completing the Square With Leading Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051078 #edb/ref 1033 3824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051078 #edb/ref 1034 1.20001426448432 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051078 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7f1179a4-dcfa-50b2-b12b-655dfefa3ff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171351 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d5f2036a-0fe0-50c3-b684-fe037208bf4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171352 #edb/ref 1080 "An empty set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a26ebc2d-f69b-5be5-a62d-fe9979a136f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171353 #edb/ref 1080 "A circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171353 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171353 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a7226782-b0b8-5d75-83ac-7f6e8629c23f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171354 #edb/ref 1080 "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171354 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171354 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c22bc215-d13d-59ed-b376-a61232d50fae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171355 #edb/ref 1080 "A parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ab9b53c-e1b2-5002-844a-b3352768d4b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171356 #edb/ref 1080 "An hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171356 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171356 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "575bdd47-ebf1-53ed-9f53-e33ef9a63b4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186557061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171369 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "39cdf548-2580-5d02-8060-91eecdc48cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171834 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171834 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n![](images/3b/3bf74b3f4c11508b9daa66464cc451933f5637c22ffeaaae303378fca2571893.png)\nand the curve $\\mathcal C,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+ 2x_2^2 -2x_1+2x_2 =7 .\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $5y_1^2-y_2^2,$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $\\mathcal C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171834 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ecd84f8-5157-595a-93b6-a865acaa53d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171834 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\: \\underbrace{ 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2}_{Q(\\mathbf{x})} -2x_1+2x_2 =7\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/78/783b32a7595534982e5fb057c049081564807b9d47988670c7d30058fb3900a0.png)\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(\\mathbf x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable $y_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ 5{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186171835 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171835 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ![](images/e6/e60bf9596889dbea2f6719078ad7817e12f96063cae887630abdd448e132437e.svg)-plane?\n\nI. ![](images/3d/3da52050877a2855dba7355e4bbdd91e95ad7cd31fe62ff8d5d0c6ca895f6589.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/b0/b0aa9bc434b0d9350630088f346e42243e24219ee4086b0b3563e6cc64bd552f.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/17/17750e29fd96388982c719635201ad4788c2c6d40352b96d3daba019fce8db12.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c03c6fbb-176a-5f30-b928-73908cb93277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171835 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.  \n\n- The equation ![](images/3d/3da52050877a2855dba7355e4bbdd91e95ad7cd31fe62ff8d5d0c6ca895f6589.svg) defines a non-empty set. It represents the point   ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) in the ![](images/e1/e15fa72f0cdfc4bb75a38b1aa53e99baad6ed04b742e19b31d9c80f809bbd26e.svg)-plane.\n\n- The equation ![](images/b0/b0aa9bc434b0d9350630088f346e42243e24219ee4086b0b3563e6cc64bd552f.svg) defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ![](images/8c/8c33e6564649c3beca9591e67622629ffab451061b8a92931451ef854d188bcd.svg) and ![](images/f0/f0b10bbcfd13006bc3490f782396e26677fa5c329dd8a770c828aa9ca9011eb3.svg) while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ![](images/17/17750e29fd96388982c719635201ad4788c2c6d40352b96d3daba019fce8db12.svg) defines a non-empty set. It represents a  pair of intersecting lines, ![](images/01/012aab369190f18204bc3b4530eb5fd7eed60908a2fa25a7cfd3dac1f9d76402.svg) and ![](images/ee/eefefdeef49074edb3f06e7072ba2fc144ade0c05c90510b72b0c8d4eb0871ed.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171836 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8381" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171836 #edb/ref 1071 "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3x_1^2 + 2x_1 - x_2 =\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58f6a3e7-e867-576a-ad84-0debbbc38d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171836 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we complete the square for the variable $x_1\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & ={x}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2} & ={y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive $y_2$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172028 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172029 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186557330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172030 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1045 "Classifying Quadratic Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172167 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "81dd1d17-811a-52af-9ad3-b3247253617b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172621 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172621 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172621 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172621 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172621 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186172621 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba048324-ec24-5cc4-9fc3-2455f8f53e00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172621 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})-2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2}+25)-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2}-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})^{2}}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis defines an empty set, also called an imaginary ellipse. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186172624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172624 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following equations defines two parallel lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fe38749-b027-5e25-83ce-123ba8294cb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172624 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=7.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines two parallel lines, ${x}_{2}=\\sqrt{7}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\sqrt{7},$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define two parallel lines:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=2{x}_{1}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477715 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477716 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477717 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$\n- **III.** ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "098d9a26-30b9-593f-93e1-d543e66fd2ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172625 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$ defines a non-empty set. It represents two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172885 #edb/ref 1061 31225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cf7cde9-4168-5bb5-a77b-f1c02aa56436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172886 #edb/ref 1061 31226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2f2a127-b40d-5b8f-ba64-4a1cad0e08c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172887 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172887 #edb/ref 1061 31227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e347e125-d8fa-58ef-8dc4-81fc67d191f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172888 #edb/ref 1061 31229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3eb3af03-a01d-5d40-94ed-828350b2cacf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172889 #edb/ref 1061 31230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8393be76-0567-5bf7-bd97-bfad1ef56506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172891 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172891 #edb/ref 1061 31358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172891 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea16c9e4-6358-52d5-9437-e5c11b9cdeec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172891 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172919 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172919 #edb/ref 1061 32209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172919 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e64c954-a22d-5a07-9a29-b00dae41d7a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172919 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172920 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172920 #edb/ref 1061 32210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172920 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4b30b5f-3e46-516c-bdb2-28d2099fba5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1061 32211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "980cb0f2-0635-5e7b-926e-f8cbd302bf7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1064 "The general equation of a second order (quadratic) curve is an expression of the form \n\n$$\n\\alpha_{11}x_1^2+2\\alpha_{12}x_1x_2 + \\alpha_{22}x_2^2 + 2\\alpha_{13}x_1+ 2\\alpha_{23}x_2 +\\alpha_{33}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere  $x_1$ and $x_2$ are variables, and $\\alpha_{ij} \\in \\Bbb R$ for all $1\\leq i,j \\leq 3.$ \n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n3x_1^2-x_1x_2-5x_2^2+5x_2 - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nis a general quadratic curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1065 5004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2d9b3527-1999-5169-affa-96a37f99fb01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n  where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal  eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables\n![](images/ab/ab1ecd406bcaf2f48f60d0e97c214f1fae9ab911b08ebe2bd74a6e43e0aa524b.svg)\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/18/1820ce650da02daa432023803a773821f63753e2adda1c6e55964fdddb913934.svg)\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n and, by completing the  square for the variable $y_2$,  we get the equation \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as\n![](images/2d/2d128752c7b7a73db6a3dfc8c1fc232347b01d50376b46b08704a9ebd4e3ceb7.svg)\nwhich is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which  both semiaxes are equal to $2.$      \n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1065 5005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cd5dde1f-7d5c-58d9-8674-6bf984a51d0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying a General Quadratic Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1064 "A quadratic equation with no cross-terms can be rewritten in standard form by completing the square. Let's see how this is done in the following example. \n\nSuppose we are given the equation  \n\n$$\n2x_1^2+x_2^2 - 4x_1 + 4x_2 = 2,\n$$\n\nwhat type of curve does it represent?\n\nFirst, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1065 5015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bd1f3565-37df-5db7-aa06-a573b6b58f8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying a Quadratic Curve That Does Not Contain a Cross-Product Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1064 "There are three basic so-called *non-degenerate quadratic curves*:\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Ellipse |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Hyperbola |\n| $x_2^2= 2px_1$ | Parabola |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1065 5129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "31519439-ac97-5940-b01b-de36561f9618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Degenerate Quadratic Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1064 "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Pair of\n intersecting  lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of \n parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of \n coincident lines |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1065 5130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7fa5c07c-714f-555e-9253-e8c6b56484e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1067 "Degenerate (Real) Quadratic Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary\n parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,$ which is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1065 5131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81eb37e3-202d-5934-93ce-8534c3ffc1a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1067 "Empty Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470083 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470084 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470085 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$\n- **III.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7ec5e81-6115-5881-b6f7-16bb9226da5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365010 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$ defines a non-empty set. It represents the hyperbola ${x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365017 #edb/ref 1071 "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}=-7\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59ff20d8-dfd7-5f57-8dd7-676de1c62793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365017 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1} & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4) & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variable\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2,\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365018 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa02b4c6-e130-5f57-94ca-7875e4b08f0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365019 #edb/ref 1080 "A pair of intersecting lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34b2f2de-15d8-5ab1-b00e-502c92c80ede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365020 #edb/ref 1080 "An empty set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365020 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365020 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a07a491-9472-50b7-8caa-2551ff3dffe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365021 #edb/ref 1080 "A parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365021 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365021 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d3feab1f-710d-5f7d-916d-6dccaf02ac63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365022 #edb/ref 1080 "A pair of parallel lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365022 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365022 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "626e74b3-c07c-5db1-8452-88dfd54f5e59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365023 #edb/ref 1080 "A hyperbola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365023 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365023 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "83cebc46-9365-5662-a211-861041f69667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366615 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366615 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366615 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366615 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c86266f7-94c1-52a6-b432-a64ea0eb634a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366615 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})+3\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+10{y}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1) & =8+1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366616 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "204f44fd-2c19-5e76-8874-9a7fd60fd729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366617 #edb/ref 1080 "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366617 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366617 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ff7e060b-1316-5b07-b470-df5ef86724ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366618 #edb/ref 1080 "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366618 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366618 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3c4007f-a76f-5450-9d48-38785eca112a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366619 #edb/ref 1080 "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366619 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366619 #edb/ref 10